{
  "root_cause": "In code_snippets_v2/lazy_init_c/client.py the module-level variable _client_cfg is set once at import with _client_cfg = get_config(). This captures a reference to the dictionary object returned by config.get_config() at import time. Meanwhile code_snippets_v2/lazy_init_c/config.py.reset_config() sets the module-level _config to None so that subsequent get_config() calls create and return a new dict. Because client._client_cfg was bound to the old dict, client functions continue to read from that stale dict rather than the new one created after reset_config().",
  "failure_mechanism": "Example sequence: when the process starts, config.get_config() creates {'api_key': 'default-key', 'base_url': 'https://api.example.com'}; client._client_cfg is assigned to that dict at import. handler.make_request('ping') returns values based on client._client_cfg. If tests call config.reset_config() and then config.set_config('api_key', 'test-key') (which populates a newly created dict), the new config lives in config._config, but client._client_cfg still points to the original dict. As a result, handler.make_request('ping') continues to return the original api_key/base_url (e.g. 'default-key'), demonstrating stale configuration despite reset_config() and set_config() being called.",
  "broken_invariant": "Handler-level calls must reflect the current configuration as returned from config.get_config(): after calling config.reset_config() and then updating configuration (e.g. via set_config), calling handler.make_request(endpoint) must return values equal to the current get_config() values (i.e. get_base_url() + '/' + endpoint and api_key == get_config()['api_key']). Currently this invariant is violated because client functions read from a snapshot dict captured at import time.",
  "fix_strategy": "Remove the import-time capture of the config dict in client.py and instead fetch the current config at call time. Concretely, I removed the module-level _client_cfg = get_config() binding and updated get_api_key() and get_base_url() to call get_config() each time (via a small helper _get_client_cfg()). This ensures client functions always read the dict currently returned by config.get_config(), so a config.reset_config() that causes a fresh dict to be created will be observed by the client and therefore by handler.make_request().",
